---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "6 Best Cordless Drills for Home Use in 2026: Tested & Ranked";
const description =
  "We tested lightweight cordless drills, compact 12V-class options, and combo kits built for casual homeowners, not contractors. No torque-spec homework required. Only 6 earned a spot on this list.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "craftsman-v20-drill-impact-combo",
    name: "Craftsman V20 Cordless Drill/Driver & Impact Driver Combo Kit (CMCK200C2)",
    category: "20V, Brushed, Drill + Impact Driver Combo Kit",
    badge: "Best Overall Combo Kit",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 5200,
    pros: [
      "Combo kit gives you a drill/driver and an impact driver in one box, covering nearly everything a casual homeowner ever needs to build or fix",
      "Ships with two batteries and a charger, so there is nothing extra to buy before your first project",
      "Straightforward two-speed gearbox and a single clutch dial keep operation simple for anyone who does not want to think about torque settings",
      "Compact enough that both tools sit comfortably in one hand for overhead work like hanging curtain rods or a ceiling fixture",
      "Price undercuts nearly every combo kit from the premium pro brands by a wide margin",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Brushed motor means shorter runtime and a bit more heat under sustained use than the brushless options on this list, though that rarely matters for occasional home projects",
      "Plastic housing feels noticeably less substantial than the metal-reinforced pro-tier tools, so this is not a kit built for daily jobsite abuse",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "For a homeowner starting from zero who wants one purchase that handles furniture assembly, shelving, and the occasional deck screw without needing to understand torque specs, this combo kit is the easiest recommendation on this list.",
    description:
      "Most casual homeowners do not need two separate purchasing decisions, they need one box that solves the problem, and that is exactly what this Craftsman combo kit is built for. It pairs a basic drill/driver with an impact driver, so the moment you graduate from assembling a bookshelf to driving a few long deck screws into a fence rail, you already own the right tool instead of forcing the drill to do a job it struggles with. Setup is genuinely simple: charge the two included batteries, pick a clutch setting, and go. There is no twenty-one-position clutch to puzzle over and no separate battery or charger to track down and buy before you can use the thing, which is where a lot of first-time buyers get tripped up on bare-tool purchases from the bigger pro brands. In our testing it handled everything a typical weekend project throws at it, from driving shelf brackets into studs to assembling flat-pack furniture, without ever feeling like it was working hard. The brushed motor is the honest trade-off versus the brushless picks on this list; it runs a little hotter and the batteries do not stretch quite as far per charge, but for a drill that comes out a handful of times a month rather than every day, that difference is close to irrelevant. Build quality is clearly aimed at a homeowner price point rather than a contractor one, with more plastic in the housing than you would find on a premium tool, so we would not hand this to someone drilling all day, every day. For the actual use case it is built for, it is close to unbeatable value.",
  },
  {
    id: "black-decker-20v-max-drill-driver",
    name: "Black+Decker 20V MAX Cordless Drill/Driver Kit (LDX120C)",
    category: "20V MAX, Brushed, Lightweight Single Drill",
    badge: "Best Lightweight Single Drill",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 12600,
    pros: [
      "Among the lightest full-size cordless drills we tested, genuinely comfortable for one-handed use hanging curtain rods or shelves overhead",
      "Simple 11-position clutch is easy to understand at a glance rather than an intimidating wall of numbers",
      "Ships as a complete kit with battery and charger at one of the lowest price points in this category",
      "Small enough to store in a kitchen drawer rather than needing dedicated garage shelf space",
      "Long track record as a reliable first drill, with a huge base of long-term homeowner reviews to back it up",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Torque is on the low end, so it will bog down on larger spade-bit holes or dense hardwood rather than power through them",
      "Single-speed range means less fine control than the two and three-speed drills on this list",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The drill to buy if your entire project list is furniture, shelves, and picture hanging and you simply want the lightest, cheapest, no-nonsense option that will not sit heavy in a drawer for years.",
    description:
      "There is a specific kind of buyer this drill is built for: someone who needs a cordless drill maybe six times a year, does not want to spend more than a casual dinner out, and cares far more about the tool being light and simple than about chasing a torque number. The LDX120C nails that brief. At well under three pounds, it is noticeably easier to hold overhead for a curtain rod or a closet shelf than any full-size 18V drill we tested, and that lighter weight matters more than most spec sheets let on, since the number one reason a heavy pro-tier drill ends up sitting unused in a drawer is that it is genuinely tiring to use one-handed above your head. The clutch has eleven settings rather than the twenty-plus found on premium drills, and honestly, that is a feature for this buyer, not a limitation, since fewer options means less second-guessing about which number to pick before driving a screw into drywall anchors or trim. Torque tops out on the modest side, so boring large holes through thick lumber or driving long structural screws will make the motor work audibly harder than it should, and this is not the drill for deck building or fence repair beyond light patch work. But for the actual jobs a casual homeowner reaches for a drill to do, assembling a dresser, mounting a towel bar, hanging a mirror, it does the job without drama, costs little enough that buyer's remorse is not really a risk, and comes as a complete kit so there is no separate battery purchase to plan around.",
  },
  {
    id: "makita-12v-cxt-drill-driver",
    name: "Makita 12V max CXT Cordless Driver-Drill Kit (FD05R1)",
    category: "12V-Class, Brushless, Compact Drill",
    badge: "Best Compact 12V-Class Drill",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 2900,
    pros: [
      "Genuinely tiny and light, easily the most comfortable drill on this list for extended one-handed and overhead use",
      "Brushless motor punches well above its compact size for typical home tasks like shelving, curtain rods, and furniture assembly",
      "Short head length reaches into tight cabinet corners and between closely spaced studs better than any full-size drill here",
      "Compact battery recharges quickly and holds its charge well between the occasional weekend project",
      "Feels like a premium tool in hand despite its small footprint, with none of the rattle some budget compacts have",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Torque ceiling is the lowest of the brushless options on this list, so it is not the choice for anything beyond light-duty drilling and driving",
      "A separate battery platform from Makita's full-size 18V LXT tools, so it will not share batteries if you later add an 18V tool from the brand",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If you have handled a full-size drill and thought it felt heavier and clunkier than a task like hanging a shelf should require, this is the answer. A legitimate main recommendation for light home use, not just a footnote pick.",
    description:
      "Compact 12V-class drills often get treated as a secondary tool that professionals carry alongside their real drill, but for a casual homeowner who never needs to bore through engineered lumber, this Makita compact deserves to be the only drill in the house rather than an afterthought. It weighs next to nothing, which sounds like a minor detail until you have spent ten minutes holding a full-size drill over your head while mounting a curtain rod bracket, at which point the difference becomes very real. Despite the small size, the brushless motor is genuinely capable for the tasks a homeowner actually does: driving screws into drywall anchors, assembling flat-pack furniture, drilling pilot holes for picture hooks, and running down cabinet hardware, all without feeling underpowered. The short head length is a real practical advantage too, letting the chuck get into tight spaces behind furniture or inside a cabinet that a full-size drill body simply cannot reach. Where it draws a clear line is torque: this is not the tool for large-diameter holes, dense hardwood, or long deck screws, and pushing it into that kind of work will bog the motor down quickly rather than power through. It also uses Makita's compact battery platform rather than the brand's mainstream 18V LXT batteries, so if you eventually pick up an 18V Makita tool, the batteries will not be interchangeable. For anyone whose actual project list tops out at furniture, shelving, and small fixes, though, it is a genuinely excellent, comfortable tool that never feels like it is fighting you.",
  },
  {
    id: "dewalt-12v-max-drill-driver",
    name: "DeWalt 12V MAX Cordless Drill/Driver Kit (DCD710S2)",
    category: "12V-Class, Brushed, Compact Drill",
    badge: "Best for One-Handed Overhead Work",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 4100,
    pros: [
      "Exceptionally well balanced for its size, making sustained overhead work like ceiling fixtures or curtain rods noticeably less tiring than with a full-size drill",
      "Two-speed gearbox gives a helpful bit of extra control over a single-speed compact drill without adding real bulk",
      "Compact enough to store in a small toolbox or even a kitchen junk drawer between projects",
      "Recognizable, widely available brand, so replacement batteries and chargers are easy to find years down the road",
      "Priced well below DeWalt's full-size 20V MAX lineup while still carrying the brand's build reputation",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Brushed motor and 12V-class torque mean it will struggle with anything beyond light drilling and driving, including most deck or fence repair work",
      "A separate battery platform from DeWalt's mainstream 20V MAX tools, so it will not share batteries with a 20V MAX drill or impact driver added later",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A smart pick for anyone who specifically wants a well-known, trusted brand name in a genuinely lightweight package, without paying for or hauling around 20V MAX bulk they will never use for occasional home tasks.",
    description:
      "DeWalt's reputation was built on full-size, jobsite-grade tools, but this 12V MAX compact drill shows the brand also understands that a huge share of buyers simply need something light, simple, and trustworthy for occasional home use. The balance is the standout quality here: the battery and motor are positioned so the tool does not feel front-heavy, which becomes noticeable the moment you are holding it overhead to mount a smoke detector bracket or a light fixture and your wrist is not fighting the tool's weight the whole time. The two-speed gearbox is a small but genuinely useful addition over a single-speed compact drill, letting you switch to a slower, more controlled range for driving screws into drywall anchors and a faster range for drilling pilot holes, without adding meaningful bulk or weight to the tool itself. In our testing it handled the bread-and-butter tasks of home DIY, furniture assembly, shelf mounting, curtain hardware, small repairs, without hesitation, and the brushed motor, while less efficient than the brushless compacts on this list, still delivered consistent performance across a normal weekend project. It is not a tool for anything beyond light-duty work; boring larger holes or driving long screws into dense lumber will make the motor labor audibly, and it is worth knowing upfront that this 12V MAX battery will not power any of DeWalt's mainstream 20V MAX tools if you expand your collection later. As a dedicated, trusted-brand compact drill for light home tasks, it earns its spot.",
  },
  {
    id: "ryobi-one-plus-drill-driver-kit",
    name: "Ryobi ONE+ 18V Cordless 1/2-inch Drill/Driver Kit (P1811)",
    category: "18V ONE+, Brushed, Budget Full-Size Drill",
    badge: "Best Value Full-Size Kit",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 18400,
    pros: [
      "Full-size 1/2-inch chuck and 24-position clutch handle a wider range of home projects than the compact drills on this list",
      "Battery is compatible with Ryobi's enormous ONE+ ecosystem of over 300 tools, useful if a lawn tool or vacuum from the brand comes next",
      "Ships as a complete kit with battery and charger at a genuinely low price for a full-size drill",
      "Simple two-speed gearbox is easy to learn without a manual",
      "One of the most widely available and reviewed drills in this entire category, so support and accessories are never hard to find",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Brushed motor and full-size body mean more weight in hand than the compact 12V-class drills on this list during long overhead sessions",
      "Build quality is aimed at homeowner-tier use rather than daily jobsite abuse, with more plastic than the premium brands",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If you want a full-size drill with real bit-holding capacity and clutch range, but still at a homeowner-friendly price and with a battery platform that can grow into other yard and garage tools, this is the smart choice.",
    description:
      "Ryobi has become the default answer for homeowners who want more capability than the smallest compact drills offer without paying pro-brand prices, and this ONE+ kit is a clean example of why. The full-size 1/2-inch chuck accepts a wider range of bit shanks than the compact drills on this list, and the 24-position clutch gives real, fine-grained control over driving depth, useful when you are setting a lot of cabinet hardware or working with a mix of drywall, softwood, and trim in the same afternoon. In our testing it comfortably handled deck screws, shelving hardware, furniture assembly, and general drilling into wood and drywall, the exact mix of tasks that make up the overwhelming majority of homeowner drill use. The two-speed gearbox switches cleanly between a controlled range for driving and a faster range for drilling, and setup could not be simpler for a first-time buyer since everything needed is in the box. The trade-off compared to the compact 12V-class picks on this list is weight and bulk: this is a full-size drill, and after a long stretch of overhead work it is noticeably more tiring on the wrist than the Makita or DeWalt compacts here. Build quality leans toward homeowner-grade rather than jobsite-rugged, with a mostly plastic housing, but for the price and the fact that the battery plugs into Ryobi's enormous ONE+ tool lineup, including string trimmers, vacuums, and shop tools, it is a genuinely smart first purchase if you expect to add more cordless tools to the garage over time.",
  },
  {
    id: "skil-pwrcore-12-drill-driver",
    name: "Skil PWRCore 12 Brushless Drill Driver Kit (DL529402)",
    category: "12V-Class, Brushless, Compact Drill",
    badge: "Best Compact Brushless Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 3300,
    pros: [
      "Brushless motor at a compact-drill price point, unusual for this category and genuinely noticeable in how efficiently it uses each charge",
      "Light and well-proportioned for one-handed use, comparable in feel to compacts costing meaningfully more",
      "Simple single dial for clutch and drill mode keeps the learning curve close to zero",
      "Battery holds its charge well between infrequent uses, which matters more for a drill that spends most of its life in a drawer",
      "Genuinely quiet in operation, a small but real perk if you are drilling near a sleeping baby or in a shared-wall apartment",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Skil's accessory and battery ecosystem is much smaller than the major brands, so there are fewer other tools to expand into on the same battery",
      "Torque is modest even by 12V-class standards, so it is best kept to light drilling and driving rather than anything demanding",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best-value brushless option on this list for a homeowner who wants the efficiency and longevity of a brushless motor without paying full price for the badge on the side of the tool.",
    description:
      "Brushless motors have historically been treated as a premium feature reserved for pricier drills, and this Skil compact is one of the clearest signs that is no longer true. For a price close to some brushed compact drills, you get a genuinely brushless motor, which means it runs cooler, wastes less energy as heat, and gets meaningfully more work out of each battery charge than a comparable brushed tool, a real advantage for a drill that might sit unused for weeks between projects and needs to still have a useful charge when you finally grab it. In hand it is light and nicely proportioned, comfortable to hold one-handed for the kind of overhead work, mounting a curtain rod bracket or a smoke detector, that makes a heavier drill exhausting after just a few minutes. Controls are about as simple as this category gets: one dial handles both the clutch setting and switching into drill mode, so there is genuinely nothing to puzzle over before your first project. The obvious limitation is Skil's much smaller tool lineup compared to Ryobi, DeWalt, or Makita, so if you are hoping to build out a matching battery platform of yard tools or a vacuum down the line, the options are thinner. Torque is also on the lower end even among the compact drills here, so this stays a light-duty tool for furniture, shelving, and small fixes rather than anything approaching deck or fence repair. As a dedicated, efficient, no-fuss drill for occasional home use, it punches well above its price.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Cordless Drill for Home Use",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-cordless-drill-for-home-use/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best cordless drill for home use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most casual homeowners, the Craftsman V20 Drill/Driver and Impact Driver Combo Kit is our top pick because it covers both drilling and heavier fastening in one affordable, easy-to-use kit. If you want the lightest possible option for overhead work, the Makita 12V max CXT compact drill is the most comfortable tool on this list, and the Ryobi ONE+ P1811 kit is the best value if you want a full-size drill with room to grow into a wider battery-powered toolkit.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I really need an 18V drill, or is a compact 12V-class drill enough for a homeowner?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For the vast majority of homeowner tasks, furniture assembly, hanging shelves and curtain rods, small repairs, and drilling into wood or drywall, a compact 12V-class drill has plenty of torque and is meaningfully lighter and easier to use one-handed. Step up to a full-size 18V or 20V drill only if your projects regularly include deck or fence repair, larger-diameter holes, or driving longer screws into dense lumber.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I buy a single drill or a drill and impact driver combo kit?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A combo kit is the smarter buy for most homeowners because it is usually priced close to a single drill kit while adding an impact driver that handles longer screws and lag bolts far more easily than a drill alone. If your projects genuinely never go beyond light furniture assembly and small fixes, a single lightweight drill is enough, but a combo kit future-proofs you for deck repairs, fence work, or anything requiring more fastening power.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much should a homeowner expect to spend on a good cordless drill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A genuinely good cordless drill for home use, complete with a battery and charger, typically costs between 40 and 130 dollars for a single drill kit, and 90 to 180 dollars for a drill and impact driver combo kit. You do not need to spend pro-tier prices of 150 dollars and up for a bare tool plus separate batteries to get a drill that comfortably handles everyday homeowner projects.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a cheap cordless drill without a proper clutch going to strip my screws?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, this is a common and avoidable problem. A drill without an adjustable clutch, or with only one or two crude power settings, will keep driving at full force after a screw is seated, which strips screw heads in drywall and can blow through the surface of trim or furniture. Every drill on this list includes an adjustable clutch with enough range to prevent that, which is one of the most important features to check before buying, even on a budget drill.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a hammer drill or a pro-grade drill for occasional home projects?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No, not for the kind of light, occasional projects most homeowners tackle. A hammer drill is only necessary if you are regularly drilling into brick, block, or concrete, and a pro-grade high-torque drill is built for daily jobsite use that most homeowners never come close to. For masonry work or heavier contractor-style projects, our full best cordless drills guide covers hammer drills and pro-tier options in depth.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-17",
      dateModified: "2026-08-17",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | The Honest Reviewers`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-17",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-17",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "cordless drill for home use",
      "cordless drill for homeowners",
      "lightweight cordless drill",
      "beginner cordless drill",
      "cordless drill combo kit",
      "12V drill",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Cordless Drills for Home Use</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Not a contractor. Not drilling into concrete every week. Just furniture, shelves, and the occasional weekend fix. We tested lightweight drills, compact 12V-class options, and combo kits to find the ones that actually make sense for a homeowner, without the torque-spec homework.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#craftsman-v20-drill-impact-combo"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#need-pro-drill"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Do I Need a Pro Drill?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 17, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="6.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#need-pro-drill" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Do You Actually Need a Pro-Grade Drill?</a>
          <a href="#what-matters" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Matters for Home & Light DIY Use</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#combo-vs-single" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Combo Kit vs a Single Drill</a>
          <a href="#what-you-can-do" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. What a Home-Use Drill Can (and Can't) Do</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
