          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight and Ergonomics for One-Handed, Overhead Work</h3>
          <p>
            Most homeowner drilling happens above shoulder height, mounting a curtain rod bracket, a smoke detector, a shelf, or a light fixture, often while holding the item in place with your other hand. A drill's weight matters enormously here in a way it simply does not for a contractor bracing a drill against their hip while framing a wall. We paid close attention to how each drill felt after several continuous minutes of one-handed, overhead use, not just its listed weight on a spec sheet, since balance affects perceived heaviness as much as raw ounces do.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Simple Clutch Settings, Not More Clutch Settings</h3>
          <p>
            A twenty-one-position clutch is a genuine feature for a finish carpenter who needs to fine-tune driving depth across dozens of cabinet screws a day. For a homeowner assembling a bookshelf twice a year, it is an intimidating wall of numbers that adds friction to a task that should be simple. We favored drills with a clear, easy-to-understand clutch range, and did not penalize a drill for having fewer settings if those settings covered the tasks a homeowner actually does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Combo Kit Value Over Buying Pieces Separately</h3>
          <p>
            A drill alone eventually runs into a job it is not built for, usually a longer deck screw or a stubborn lag bolt, and trying to force it through with a standard drill/driver is slow and frustrating. A combo kit that pairs a drill with an impact driver solves that before it becomes a problem, and because combo kits are often priced close to a single drill kit, they tend to be the better value even if you are not sure yet whether you will need the impact driver.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Shelf Life Matters More Than Runtime</h3>
          <p>
            Spec sheets love to advertise runtime, how many screws a battery drives on a single charge, but that number matters far more to someone using a drill eight hours a day than to a homeowner who might not touch the tool for six weeks between projects. What matters more for infrequent use is self-discharge, how much charge a battery loses simply sitting on a shelf. A drill that shows up dead the one Saturday a month you actually need it is a worse experience than one with slightly shorter per-charge runtime but a battery that holds its charge reliably for months. We factored this in when picking compact and combo drills for this list, favoring modern lithium-ion packs with low self-discharge over older or lower-quality cells.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lower Price Point Without a Race to the Bottom</h3>
          <p>
            None of the six drills on this list ask you to spend pro-tier money, and several are priced low enough that a first-time buyer can pick one up without much deliberation. But cheap and good are not the same thing, and we excluded drills with genuinely unreliable clutches, chucks prone to slipping, or reviews suggesting they fail within a season of light use. The goal was affordable and dependable, not simply the lowest number on the shelf.
          </p>
