          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Heavy Pro-Tier Drill That Sits in a Drawer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is tempting to buy the most powerful drill on the shelf just in case, but a heavy contractor-grade drill is genuinely tiring to use overhead, and that fatigue is exactly why so many end up sitting unused after the novelty wears off. Match the drill's weight to how you will actually use it, not to a hypothetical future project that may never happen.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Bare Tool Without Realizing You Need a Battery and Charger</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Professional-tier drills are frequently sold as a bare tool, assuming the buyer already owns compatible batteries from other tools on the same platform. A first-time buyer can easily miss this and end up needing to spend another 60 to 100 dollars just to actually use the drill they bought. Every drill on this list ships as a complete kit specifically to avoid that surprise.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Clutch and Stripping Screws</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheap drill without a proper adjustable clutch keeps driving at full force after a screw is seated, which strips screw heads in drywall and can blow straight through the surface of trim or furniture. Check that a drill has a genuine adjustable clutch, not just an on-off trigger, before buying, even on a tight budget.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Two Tools From the Same Brand on Different Battery Voltages</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many brands sell more than one battery platform, for example a compact 12V-class line alongside a mainstream 18V or 20V lineup, and these batteries are almost never interchangeable. Buying a compact drill and, later, a full-size tool from the same brand without checking the voltage can leave you maintaining two separate battery ecosystems instead of one. Check the voltage class before assuming a second tool from a familiar brand will share your existing battery.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

